# Tor configuration for Shadow IRC.
#
# Append to /etc/tor/torrc, then: sudo systemctl restart tor
#
# Publishes the web client as an onion service, so the network is reachable
# without a domain, a host, or an IP that anyone can point at.
#
# The daemon makes no outbound requests of its own — moderation runs in
# process — so there is nothing here to proxy outward.

# ---- onion service ---------------------------------------------------------
# Serves the same local port the Cloudflare tunnel does. Running both is fine
# and intended: the tunnel is the convenient address, the onion is the one that
# survives losing the domain.
#
# Port 80 (not 443) because Tor already authenticates and encrypts the
# connection — there is no certificate and no TLS inside an onion service. The
# web client detects this and uses ws:// rather than wss://.
HiddenServiceDir /var/lib/tor/shadow-irc/
HiddenServicePort 80 127.0.0.1:8888

# Only v3 onions, and no relaying — this box serves its own service and nothing
# else. It never becomes an exit or a relay for other people's traffic.
HiddenServiceVersion 3

# Nothing here needs outbound Tor, so SOCKS stays on loopback and unused.
SocksPort 127.0.0.1:9050

# After restarting tor, read the address with:
#   sudo cat /var/lib/tor/shadow-irc/hostname
#
# Back up /var/lib/tor/shadow-irc/ somewhere safe. Those keys ARE the address:
# lose them and the onion address is gone permanently and cannot be recreated.
